## answer
answer-ice-level-why = Ice floats, so the buoyant force equals its weight: it pushes aside water of exactly its own mass. Melted, the ice becomes water of the same mass — melting does not change the mass — and water of that mass takes exactly the room the ice pushed aside. So the level stays. Ice lying on land (glaciers) pushes aside no water — melted, it adds water to the ocean, and the level rises.
answer-ice-level-say = The level did not move by a mark!
answer-ice-level-text = Without ice the water stood at { $value }.{" "}
answer-ice-level-text-2 = The floating ice pushed aside { $shown } of water — that is { $iceMass }: the displaced water has the mass of the ice (its weight balances the cube's — Archimedes). The cube itself takes more room — ≈ { $iceVolume }, as ice is less dense than water; ≈ { $under } % of its volume was under water.
answer-ice-level-text-3 = Floating ice pushes aside water of its own mass (Archimedes); ≈ { $under } % of the cube's volume is under water, as ice is less dense than water.
answer-ice-level-text-4 = { $iceMass } of ice became { $iceMass } of water, and { $iceMass } of water take exactly { $shown }
answer-ice-level-text-5 = the meltwater has the mass of the displaced water, and so its volume
answer-ice-level-text-6 = { $value3 }With the cube — { $value }. { $shown } Once the ice melted — { $value2 }. Melting does not change the mass: { $iceMass } — just as much as the cube pushed aside. So the level stays; melting sea ice does not raise the ocean either — unlike glaciers on land.
answer-resistance-temperature-why = A current in a metal is the ordered motion of free electrons through a crystal lattice of ions. The electrons keep bumping into the ions and giving them energy — that is the resistance. As the metal warms, the ions vibrate harder and get in the electrons’ way more often, so the resistance grows. Near room temperature almost evenly: by the same share α per degree, for copper ≈ 0.4 % per degree.
answer-resistance-temperature-say = Warmed by { $t } — the resistance grew: α ≈ { $alpha } per degree!
answer-resistance-temperature-text = { $list }. The hotter the water (and the copper wire in it), the larger the coil’s resistance. The temperature coefficient of resistance α = (R − R₀) / (R₀·Δt) = ({ $R } − { $R2 }) / ({ $R2 } · { $t }) ≈ { $alpha } 1/°C, and the table gives { $table } 1/°C for copper: the resistance grows by about { $alpha2 } % per degree. A glowing lamp filament does the same, only much more.
answer-electrochemical-equivalent-mg-c = mg/C
answer-electrochemical-equivalent-why = A current in a solution is ions moving. Each copper ion Cu²⁺ that reaches the cathode takes two electrons from it and becomes a copper atom that stays on the plate. So as much charge as has passed, so many atoms have settled: the mass is proportional to the charge, m = k·q = k·I·t. The coefficient k depends only on the substance — on its molar mass M and the ion’s charge z: k = M/(z·F), F being the charge of a mole of electrons. And the copper anode dissolves: each of its atoms gives up two electrons and goes into the solution as an ion — so the solution does not run short of copper.
answer-electrochemical-equivalent-say = k ≈ { $k } — each coulomb puts this much copper on the cathode!
answer-electrochemical-equivalent-text = The cathode weighed m₁ = { $before }, after the experiment m₂ = { $after }: Δm = { $dm } of copper settled on it. A current of I ≈ { $q } A flowed for { $t }, so a charge I·t ≈ { $q2 } C passed. k = Δm / (I·t) ≈ { $k }; the table gives { $table } for copper. This is Faraday’s law: each Cu²⁺ ion takes two electrons at the cathode, so k = M / (2·F), F ≈ 96 485 C/mol. The copper anode meanwhile dissolves — as much copper goes into the solution as settles on the cathode. The balance reads hundredths of a gram, so the more charge, the more precise k.
answer-word-sinks = sank
answer-word-inside = floated inside the water
answer-word-up = came up
answer-floating-conditions-why = Two forces act on a body under water: gravity m·g = ρ_body·V·g pulls down, the buoyant force ρ_water·V·g pushes up. Both are proportional to the same volume V, so only the densities are compared. A denser body — gravity wins, and it sinks. Equal densities — the forces balance, and it stays where it was let go. Less dense — the buoyant force wins, and the body comes up until so much is out of the water that the part under it pushes aside just its own mass of water. That is why a heavy steel ship floats: it is hollow, and its mean density is less than water’s.
answer-floating-conditions-say = Sinking or floating — the density decides!
answer-text-runs = { $k }) m = { $masses }, ρ = m / V ≈ { $rho } { $cmp } ρ of water — the tube { $word }
answer-floating-conditions-text = The tube’s volume is from run 1, when it lay on the bottom: V = { $vol }. Pouring sand out changed the mass, the volume stayed: { $runs }. A body sinks when its density is greater than the liquid’s, floats inside when equal, comes up when less: then the buoyant force on the whole body under water is greater than gravity. The tube that came up is only partly under water — just enough to push aside its own mass of water. So in run 3 the cylinder would not give the volume of the whole tube.
answer-mystery-metal-why = Density belongs to a substance, not to a piece: any piece of copper, large or small, has 8.9 g/cm³, since it depends on how heavy the substance’s atoms are and how closely they are packed. So a substance can be recognised by its density. The cylinder gives the volume of an odd-shaped body because a body under water pushes aside exactly its own volume of water — that is how far the level rises.
answer-mystery-metal-say = It is { $realId }!
answer-mystery-metal-text = The block’s mass: m = m₂ − m₁ = { $dm }. Its volume is how far the water rose: V = { $dv } = { $dv2 } cm³. So ρ = m/V ≈ { $rho } g/cm³ = { $rho2 } kg/m³. The nearest density in the table is { $guess } ({ $guess2 } kg/m³). The block is indeed { $realId }. The cylinder reads the volume to 1 mL, so telling similar metals apart (copper and brass) needs a bigger block or a finer instrument.
answer-supersaturated-why = For crystals to grow, the particles need somewhere to start lining up into a regular lattice. In a clean solution cooled quietly there is no such start, and the extra substance stays dissolved — the solution is supersaturated. A tiny crystal gives a ready lattice, and the particles begin to fit onto it one by one — crystallising runs through the whole solution. It gives off the heat the solution took when the substance dissolved — so the solution warms up. That is how sodium acetate hand warmers work.
answer-supersaturated-say = Crystals — and the solution warmed up to { $peak }!
answer-supersaturated-text = At 70 °C all 20 g of sodium acetate dissolved in 4 mL of water. Cooled to { $tCool }, the water could hold only ≈ { $satCool } g, yet no crystals came out: the solution became supersaturated — it holds { $mass } times as much as the solubility allows. Crystals need a "seed" — a ready crystal to grow on. A nucleus has to gather from particles by chance in the right order, and while it is tiny it is more likely to fall apart than to grow. For sodium acetate this is especially hard: its crystal is a trihydrate, where three water molecules must be fitted in the right places next to each sodium ion, and the syrup-thick solution slows the particles down. Copper sulfate, with a simpler lattice in a runny solution, finds a nucleus almost at once (the experiment “Growing copper sulfate crystals”). A speck of dust or a scratch on the glass can act as a seed too — that is why supersaturated solutions are made in clean glassware and cooled without shaking. As soon as one was dropped in, the excess acetate crystallised (≈ { $crystalsAtPeak } g) and gave off its heat of solution (19.66 kJ/mol): the temperature jumped to { $peak }. That is how a reusable hand warmer works: a metal disc inside makes a nucleation centre, and to "recharge" the warmer it is boiled. Then the test tube slowly cools to room temperature. In the model crystallisation is instant, and without a seed the solution never crystallises by itself.
answer-heat-of-solution-line = took in
answer-heat-of-solution-line-2 = gave off
answer-heat-of-solution-line-3 = fell
answer-heat-of-solution-line-4 = rose
answer-heat-of-solution-line-5 = { $mass } g of { $component } is { $moles } mol; dissolving, it { $value2 } ≈ { $q } kJ ({ $value }{ $component2 } kJ/mol), and the temperature { $value3 } from { $start } to { $extreme }.
answer-heat-of-solution-why = Dissolving is two processes. Taking a crystal apart into ions takes energy. When water molecules gather round the ions (hydration), energy is given off. Which wins depends on the substance: for ammonium chloride more goes into breaking the crystal, so the solution cools; for sodium hydroxide hydration gives more, so the solution warms.
answer-heat-of-solution-say = One solution cools, the other warms up!
answer-heat-of-solution-text = { $value } When a substance dissolves, its crystal is broken up — that takes energy — and its ions are surrounded by water molecules — that gives energy off. Whichever wins shows on the thermometer: ammonium chloride cools the solution (first-aid cold packs work this way), sodium hydroxide heats it. Then both beakers slowly come to room temperature. The model uses the enthalpy of solution of a very dilute solution; in a real experiment the numbers differ a little. Sodium hydroxide is caustic: at school only the teacher handles it.
answer-grow-crystals-why = Copper sulfate dissolves better in hot water: its solubility grows with temperature. As the solution cools, the water can no longer hold that much — and the excess comes out as crystals. How much — the difference of the solubilities at the two temperatures times the mass of water. The slower the cooling, the bigger and more regular the crystals grow.
answer-grow-crystals-say = ≈ { $crystals } g of crystals came out!
answer-grow-crystals-text = all of it dissolved
answer-grow-crystals-text-2 = { $crystals } stayed on the bottom
answer-grow-crystals-text-3 = {" "}A little more than lay there at first: ≈ { $lost } g of water evaporated during the heating, and less water dissolves less copper sulfate.
answer-grow-crystals-text-4 = At 20 °C 100 g of water dissolves only ≈ { $value } g of copper sulfate, so at first { $crystals } stayed on the bottom. At { $hotT } °C the solubility is ≈ { $hotT2 } g — and { $value2 }. As the solution cooled, from about { $t0 } °C it became saturated, and the excess began to come out as crystals. At 20 °C the solution again holds ≈ { $mass }, and { $crystals2 } are blue crystals on the bottom.{ $lost } The slower the cooling, the larger the crystals grow. In fact copper sulfate supersaturates a little too: crystals need a nucleus to start growing. But its lattice is simple, the solution is runny, and dust and scratches on the glass serve as nuclei, so copper sulfate supersaturates by only a few degrees — while sodium acetate in a hand warmer stays liquid for weeks (the experiment “A warmer set off by a crystal”). The model counts how much crystallises, but not the crystals' size or shape, and in it copper sulfate crystallises exactly when the solution becomes saturated.
answer-mass-fraction-why = The mass fraction is the share of the solution’s mass the dissolved substance makes: w = m(salt) / m(solution). The solution is the water and the salt together, so 5 g of salt in 45 g of water is 5 / 50 = 10 %, not 5 / 45. Dissolving does not lose any mass: the scales show the sum of the water’s and the salt’s, though the salt can no longer be seen.
answer-mass-fraction-say = Exactly { $w }!
answer-mass-fraction-text = The scales showed the salt added: m(salt) = m₂ − m₁ = { $salt }. All the salt dissolved, so the solution is the water and the salt together: m(solution) = { $water } + { $dissolved } = { $water2 }. The mass fraction w = m(salt) / m(solution) = { $dissolved } / { $water2 } ≈ { $w }. A common slip is dividing by the water: { $dissolved } / { $water } ≈ { $dissolved2 }. But the fraction is taken of the whole solution. To make a solution of mass m with a fraction w, take w·m of the substance and (1 − w)·m of water.
answer-archimedes-salt-why = The buoyant force equals the weight of the liquid in the body’s volume: F = ρ_liquid·g·V. The block is the same, so V is the same, and salt water is denser than fresh — the same volume of it weighs more. So it pushes up harder. That is why swimming is easier in the sea than in a river, and in the very salty Dead Sea a person lies on the water.
answer-archimedes-salt-say = Salt water pushes harder!
answer-archimedes-salt-text = In the air the dynamometer read { $value }, in fresh water { $value2 }: the buoyant force F₁ = P − P′ = { $F1 }. With salt dissolved, the liquid got denser (≈ { $rho2 } kg/m³ instead of { $WATER }), and the dynamometer read { $value3 }: F₂ = { $F2 }. The buoyant force F = ρ·g·V is larger in a denser liquid — that is why swimming in the sea is easier than in a river.
answer-mix-hot-cold-why = Energy is not lost: as much heat as the hot water gave, the cold water got — c·m₁·(t₁ − θ) = c·m₂·(θ − t₂). The masses are equal and the substance the same, so the hot water cools by as many degrees as the cold warms: the balance is half-way. It comes out a little lower, as part of the heat goes into the vessel and the air.
answer-mix-hot-cold-say = Equilibrium at { $real }!
answer-mix-hot-cold-text = Right after mixing the water was at { $ideal } — exactly what the heat balance gives: θ = (m₁t₁ + m₂t₂) / (m₁ + m₂), since the heat the hot water gave is the heat the cold water took. But within { $t } the cold glass beaker and the air took some of the heat, and equilibrium came at { $real }. That is why a real mixture is always a little colder than the calculation; to cut the losses, labs mix in a calorimeter.
answer-hydrostatic-weighing-why = The buoyant force equals the weight of water in the body’s volume: F_A = ρ_water·g·V. So the difference of the dynamometer in air and in water gives the volume: V = F_A / (ρ_water·g) — no cylinder needed. Then the body’s density ρ = P / (g·V) = ρ_water · P / F_A: as many times as the weight exceeds the buoyant force, the body is denser than water.
answer-hydrostatic-weighing-say = It is { $l }!
answer-hydrostatic-weighing-text = In the air the block weighs P = { $P }, in water P′ = { $P2 }. The buoyant force F_A = P − P′ = { $Fa }. It equals the weight of the water pushed aside: F_A = ρ_w·g·V, so the block’s volume V = F_A / (ρ_w·g) ≈ { $Fa2 } cm³. Its mass m = P/g ≈ { $P3 } g, and its density ρ = m/V = P/F_A · ρ_w ≈ { $rho } kg/m³. The nearest in the table is { $l } ({ $density } kg/m³). The dynamometer reads to 0.1 N, so the result may be off by a few per cent (here up to ≈ { $err } %) — telling apart substances of similar density needs a finer instrument or a heavier block.
answer-ice-latent-heat-why = The warm water gives off heat, and all of it goes into the ice: first to melt it (λ·m), then to warm the meltwater to the common temperature. What is given equals what is taken (the heat balance), and λ is found from that equality. That is why the calorimeter is warmed with the water first: otherwise part of the heat would go into it, and λ would come out smaller.
answer-ice-latent-heat-text = almost as in the table
answer-ice-latent-heat-text-2 = less
answer-ice-latent-heat-text-3 = more
answer-ice-latent-heat-text-4 = The water cooled from { $t1 } to { $th } °C and gave off Q₁ = c·m_w·(t₁ − θ) ≈ { $CW } kJ. The ice first warmed to 0 °C, melted, and its water warmed to θ: warming took ≈ { $warmIce } kJ, the rest went into melting. So λ = (Q₁ − Q_warm) / m_ice ≈ { $naive } — { $value } than the table’s { $table }. Why? Not only the water gave off heat: the aluminium calorimeter, warmed up with it, cooled too and gave ≈ { $cup } kJ. Counting it as well, λ ≈ { $full }. The rest of the difference is heat exchange with the room air. That is why the lab work uses a light calorimeter and dry, crushed ice.
answer-specific-heat-metal-j = J/(kg·°C)
answer-specific-heat-metal-why = The hot metal gives heat to the water until their temperatures are equal: c_metal·m_metal·(100 − θ) = c_w·m_w·(θ − 20). A metal’s specific heat is small, so cooling by many degrees it gives little heat — and the water warms only a little. The metal’s c is found from that equality.
answer-specific-heat-metal-text = The water warmed from { $t1 } to { $th } °C and took Q = c_w·m_w·(θ − t₁) ≈ { $CW } kJ. The cylinder gave as much, cooling from { $tb } to { $th } °C. So c = Q / (m·(t₂ − θ)) ≈ { $naive } — { $value } than the table’s { $table }. Part of the heat went into the calorimeter itself — it warmed too (≈ { $cup } kJ). Counting it, c ≈ { $full }; the rest is heat exchange with the air and the cylinder cooling a little while it was carried from the boiling water.
answer-which-metal-why = The specific heat is how much heat a kilogram gives cooling by 1 °C. Copper’s is about two and a half times less than aluminium’s. A block of the same mass cooling from 100 °C gives the water less heat — and the water warms less. The same mass, a different “store of heat”: the substance sets it.
answer-which-metal-say = Aluminium holds more heat!
answer-which-metal-say-2 = Copper holds more heat!
answer-which-metal-text = The aluminium block warmed the water to { $al }, while a copper block of the same mass would warm it only to { $cu }. It is the specific heat: aluminium has c = { $cAl } J/(kg·°C), copper { $cCu }. Cooling by 1 °C, a kilogram of aluminium gives { $cAl2 } times as much heat as a kilogram of copper: the same mass, a different "store of heat".
answer-weigh-the-water-why = Mass is the amount of matter, and freezing water goes nowhere: the same molecules just line up into a crystal. So ice weighs as much as the water (though it takes more room — it is lighter than water and floats). Boiling is another thing: molecules fly off as vapour, and the mass of what is left falls.
answer-weigh-the-water-say = Mass does not vanish — it leaves as vapour!
answer-weigh-the-water-text = {" "}({ $dFreeze } evaporated while it was cooling)
answer-weigh-the-water-text-2 = At the start the scales showed { $reading }, after freezing { $reading2 }: the water turned to ice but became neither heavier nor lighter{ $dFreeze }. Ice only takes more room, because it is less dense. After boiling: { $reading3 }, { $dBoil } less. That water did not vanish — it went into the air as vapour.
answer-boil-a-litre-why = Warming the water takes the heat Q = c·m·Δt; the stove gives it at the rate P (Q = P·t). On paper t = Q / P. In fact it takes longer: the stove first heats itself and the pot, and part of the heat keeps going into the air — the hotter the water, the more. So a calculation “on paper” is always optimistic.
answer-boil-a-litre-say = Boiled in { $t }!
answer-boil-a-litre-text = {" "}The “paper” calculation, where all the hob's energy goes into the water, gives { $paper } — the real run took { $t } % longer.
answer-boil-a-litre-text-2 = A litre of water boiled in { $t }.{ $paper } The difference is the heat that went into the pot itself, into the room air and into steam: a real experiment always takes longer than the ideal calculation.
answer-lid-or-not-why = Hot water loses most heat from its surface: the vapour flying off carries the latent heat of vaporisation with it — over 2000 kJ a kilogram. A lid keeps the vapour in the pot: it settles in drops and gives the heat back, and the warm air does not escape. More heat stays in the water — and it boils sooner.
answer-lid-or-not-say = A lid keeps the heat in!
answer-lid-or-not-text = ed
answer-lid-or-not-text-2 = s
answer-lid-or-not-text-3 = With a lid the water boil{ $value2 } in { $withLid }, without one in { $without }: { $value } longer. A lid keeps the steam in: the heat that evaporation from the surface would take away stays in the pot.
answer-melt-ice-why = While the ice melts, all the heat from the room goes not into warming but into breaking the crystal: pulling water molecules off the ice lattice takes energy — 334 kJ for every kilogram (the latent heat of fusion). The temperature stands at 0 °C until the last piece melts; only then does the heat start warming the water.
answer-melt-ice-say = Did you notice the temperature stuck at zero?
answer-melt-ice-text = While the ice was melting, the thermometer showed 0 °C for about { $p } — the temperature did not change! All the heat from the room went not into warming but into breaking the ice crystals: { $LF_KJ } kJ for every kilogram (the latent heat of fusion). Only when the last piece melted did the water start to warm up.
answer-melt-tin-why = Melting is breaking the crystal lattice, and that takes energy. While the tin melts, the heat from the spirit lamp goes into exactly that, not into warming: the temperature stands at 232 °C — tin’s melting point. When it has all melted, the heat warms the now liquid tin again.
answer-melt-tin-say = Did you see the flat “shelf” on the graph?
answer-melt-tin-text = While the tin was melting, the thermometer showed { $tm } °C for about { $p } — the temperature stood still although the lamp kept heating. All the heat went into breaking the crystal lattice: { $lf } kJ for every kilogram (the latent heat of fusion of tin). On the heating graph it is a flat “shelf”; only after the last bit melted did the temperature rise again.
answer-stearic-cooling-why = Setting, a substance gives off the same heat it took when it melted: the molecules line up into a crystal and release energy. While the melt sets, that heat just covers what it gives to the air — and the temperature stands at 69 °C. When all of it has set, there is nothing more to give — and the solid cools on.
answer-stearic-cooling-say = See the flat “shelf” on the cooling curve?
answer-stearic-cooling-text = While the melt sets, the thermometer shows ≈ { $tm } °C for about { $p }: stearic acid gives its latent heat of crystallisation to the air, so its temperature does not fall. Only when it has all set does it cool again. The “shelf” on a cooling curve tells the melting point of a substance.
answer-specific-heat-water-why = We counted as if all of the heater’s energy P·t went into the water. In fact part went into warming the glass and the heater itself, part into the air. So the water got less than we thought, and dividing all the energy by m·Δt gave more than the table. To come closer one needs a calorimeter that keeps the heat.
answer-specific-heat-water-say = It came out ≈ { $c } J/(kg·°C)!
answer-specific-heat-water-text = In { $t } the heater gave E = P·t = { $P } kJ, and the water ({ $m } g) warmed by { $dT } °C. So c = P·t / (m·Δt) ≈ { $c } J/(kg·°C) — { $extra } % more than the table's { $table }. Why more: part of the heat went not into the water but into the glass, the heater and the air, yet we counted it as if all of it went into the water. The smaller the losses, the closer to the table.
answer-heater-efficiency-why = The heater gives its heat to what it touches — the water. But the water, warming up, warms the glass, and the glass and the water’s surface give heat to the air; and the heater itself has to warm up first. The hotter the water against the room, the faster it loses heat — so the efficiency is lower when heating to a higher temperature or for longer. A vessel that conducts heat poorly, a lid and a heater fully under water give a higher efficiency.
answer-heater-efficiency-say = Efficiency ≈ { $eta }!
answer-heater-efficiency-text = In { $t } the heater spent E = P·t = { $P } kJ. The water ({ $m } g) warmed by { $dT } °C and got Q = c·m·Δt = { $Q } kJ. The efficiency η = Q / E ≈ { $eta }. The rest went into warming the glass and the heater itself and into the room’s air — see «Where the energy went». The better the heater is immersed and the less heat the vessel gives away, the higher the efficiency.
answer-salt-in-pot-why = A body heats up while the heat coming in exceeds what it gives off. A hot pot gives more and more heat to the air and radiates, and the flame under it does not heat without limit — somewhere at a few hundred degrees the two flows even out. That is far below 801 °C, so the salt does not melt. In a muffle furnace everything around is hot (900 °C), the heat has nowhere to go — and the salt melts.
answer-salt-in-pot-say = You cannot melt salt on a stove!
answer-salt-in-pot-text = {" "}In a muffle furnace at 900 °C the same salt in a porcelain crucible melted in { $furnace }.
answer-salt-in-pot-text-2 = On a gas stove the salt only got to ≈ { $top } — then the losses (the air takes heat away, and the red-hot pot also radiates it) matched the heat from the flame. That is far from { $tm } °C; the pot survived — steel melts much higher.{ $furnace }
answer-freeze-water-why = Cooling a kilogram of water from 20 to 0 °C takes c·Δt ≈ 4.2 · 20 ≈ 84 kJ out of it. Turning it into ice then takes 334 kJ (the latent heat of fusion), four times more. The freezer takes heat out at about the same rate, so the freezing lasts several times longer than the cooling.
answer-freeze-water-say = Freezing takes longer than cooling!
answer-freeze-water-say-2 = There is the ice!
answer-freeze-water-text = Cooling the water to 0 °C took { $cool }, and turning it into ice another { $freeze }. To freeze, water has to give away { $LF_KJ } kJ per kilogram — as much as it takes to warm the same kilogram of water by ≈ { $degrees } °C.
answer-road-salt-why = For water to freeze, its molecules have to line up into an ice crystal. The dissolved salt ions get in the way: water molecules stay round the ions, and for ice to form after all it has to be colder. So sea water freezes not at 0 °C but at about −2 °C, and brine on the roads stays liquid in frost — the ice under it melts.
answer-road-salt-say = That is why roads are salted!
answer-text-start = below zero
answer-text-tail = {" "}That is why salt on a road turns ice into salty water that does not freeze in a moderate frost.
answer-text-more = {" "}In a colder freezer it finally froze completely.
answer-alcohol-vs-water-why = Two reasons, both for the alcohol. Its specific heat is almost half water’s (≈ 2.4 against 4.2 kJ/(kg·°C)): each degree costs less heat. And it boils at 78 °C, not 100 — fewer degrees to go. So on the same heater alcohol boils much sooner.
answer-alcohol-vs-water-say = Alcohol wins!
answer-alcohol-vs-water-text = Alcohol boiled in { $t } at { $tb } °C. The same amount of water in the same beaker would take { $tw } — { $tw2 } times longer. Two reasons: alcohol boils lower ({ $tb } °C against 100 °C), and warming it by 1 °C takes { $less } % less energy than water.
answer-sea-salt-why = Only water molecules evaporate: they leave the surface and fly into the air. Salt ions cannot do that — they stay in the solution, which gets saltier and saltier until the salt starts coming out as crystals. When all the water has gone, all the salt is left on the bottom. That is how salt is got from sea water in sunny ponds.
answer-sea-salt-say = All the salt came back!
answer-sea-salt-text = We dissolved { $salt } of salt in water, and when the water boiled away, { $crystals } of crystals were left on the bottom — all of it. Dissolving did not destroy it: it broke into particles too small to see and simply waited in the water. Salt can be won from sea water the same way.
answer-does-water-conduct-why = A current in a liquid is charged particles moving — ions. Distilled water has almost none, so a current hardly flows. Salt, dissolving, falls apart into the ions Na⁺ and Cl⁻ — charge carriers appear, and the water starts to conduct. That is why tap water and sea water conduct (they are not pure) — and why electrical things near water are dangerous.
answer-does-water-conduct-say = The salt ions carry the current!
answer-does-water-conduct-text = {" "}— { $after } times more
answer-does-water-conduct-text-2 = In distilled water the current was only { $before } — it barely conducts electricity. After adding salt the current became { $after }{ $after2 }! In water salt splits into charged particles — Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions, and they carry the charge. Tap water also contains dissolved salts and conducts — so be careful with electrical devices near water.
answer-joule-heating-why = Ions driven by the voltage keep bumping into water molecules and passing them energy — the liquid warms. How much heat — Joule–Lenz’s law tells: Q = I²·R·t, that is, all of the source’s energy spent on the current in the liquid becomes heat. The higher the voltage, the larger the current — and the faster the warming.
answer-joule-heating-say = Heated by { $dT } °C with a current!
answer-joule-heating-text = In { $time } a current of { $amps } warmed the sea water by { $dT } °C: { $q } of heat was released in the liquid. This is the Joule–Lenz law: Q = I²·R·t — twice the current heats four times as much. Immersion heaters and kettles work like this, only the current flows through a metal coil, not through the water.
answer-copper-sulfate-electrolysis-why = At the cathode the electrons go to whatever is reduced most easily. Copper ions Cu²⁺ take electrons more readily than water does (their potential is +0.34 V against a negative one for hydrogen from water) — so copper settles on the cathode, not hydrogen. At the graphite anode water gives up electrons — oxygen comes off. The solution slowly pales: its copper moves onto the cathode.
answer-copper-sulfate-electrolysis-say = The cathode turned copper-coloured!
answer-copper-sulfate-electrolysis-text = { $cu } g of copper settled on the cathode: Cu²⁺ ions take electrons and become metal (Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu). No hydrogen there — copper is reduced more easily than water (0.34 V against −0.83 V). About { $o2 } mL of oxygen formed at the anode, and sulfuric acid stays in the solution. This is how copper is refined and objects are plated — electroplating.
answer-electrolysis-why = Each hydrogen molecule H₂ forms when two hydrogen ions each take an electron at the cathode — so every molecule needs two electrons. The charge passed is q = I·t; divided by the charge of a mole of electrons (F ≈ 96 485 C) it gives the moles of electrons, and the moles of hydrogen are half that. The gas law gives the volume at room conditions.
answer-electrolysis-say = ≈ { $h2 } mL of hydrogen!
answer-electrolysis-text = In { $time } a charge of { $charge } passed through the solution, and ≈ { $h2 } of hydrogen and as much chlorine formed at the electrodes. By Faraday's law the gas is exactly proportional to the charge: twice as long or twice the current — twice the gas.
answer-oil-does-not-boil-why = Oil is a mixture of large molecules, and for them to fly off as vapour it would take a very high temperature. Before it gets there, the molecules start breaking down from the heat — smoke appears (sunflower oil’s smoke point ≈ 230 °C). So oil does not boil like water — it smokes, and can catch fire. It must never be put out with water: the water boils at once and throws the burning oil about.
answer-oil-does-not-boil-say = Oil does not boil — it smokes!
answer-oil-does-not-boil-text = The oil never boiled: at { $final } it started to smoke and break down. Water boils at 100 °C and gets no hotter, while oil breaks down before it boils — but gets much hotter. That is why we fry in oil: it heats food far hotter than boiling water.
answer-alcohol-in-freezer-why = For a substance to freeze, its molecules have to line up into a crystal, and each substance has its own temperature below which that happens. Alcohol’s molecules hold on to each other more weakly than water’s, and alcohol crystallises only at −114 °C. A freezer gives −18 °C — very far off. That is why alcohol fills thermometers for frost and windscreen fluids.
answer-alcohol-in-freezer-say = Alcohol does not mind the frost!
answer-grand-chain-why = In a solution the current is carried by ions, and they have to push their way between water molecules. In hot water the molecules move faster, the water gets “thinner” (less viscous), and the ions move more easily — the solution conducts better. So at the same voltage the current in a hot solution is larger. Metals are the other way: warmed, they conduct worse.
answer-grand-chain-say = A hot solution conducts better!
answer-grand-chain-text = In the solution at ≈ { $initial } the current was { $i1 }, in the hot one at ≈ { $initial2 } — { $i2 }: { $i22 } % more at the same voltage. The hotter the liquid, the easier its ions move — and the better it conducts.
answer-text-head = Sea water started to freeze only at { $start }, not at 0 °C like fresh water. At { $sp } °C only { $firstSolid } % of the water froze: ice forms from fresh water, and the brine left behind gets saltier and freezes ever colder — completely only below { $tE }.
answer-alcohol-in-freezer-text = The alcohol cooled to { $final } but stayed liquid: it freezes only at { $tf } °C — no home freezer gets that cold. Water would have turned to ice long ago.
